Generalized anxiety disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Excessive uncontrollable worry, Muscle tension, Sleep disturbance

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-027
Reason for Visit: Severe muscle spasms, intractable insomnia, and acute gastrointestinal distress secondary to extreme, unremitting anxiety.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted 72 hours of complete sleep deprivation, severe, painful cervical muscle spasms, and acute vomiting preventing oral hydration. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess hydration status, rule out acute cervical pathology or an acute abdomen, and provide pharmacological stabilization to prevent physiological collapse before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My neck is locked up, I haven't slept in three days because my brain won't stop, and now I'm throwing up every time I try to drink water."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of excessive, difficult-to-control worry occurring more days than not for at least six months. The worry spans multiple domains (finances, health, family). The patient is constantly restless, feeling keyed up and on edge, leading to being easily fatigued. There is chronic difficulty concentrating, with the mind frequently going blank, and irritability affecting social interactions. Over the past 72 hours, an acute exacerbation resulted in severe sleep disturbance, including a total inability to fall asleep. This led to acute somatic symptoms, specifically severe gastrointestinal distress (nausea and vomiting) today. The patient also complains of agonizing muscle tension, particularly in the neck and shoulders, which has now progressed to painful spasm. The patient exhibits an exaggerated startle response to any sudden noise.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound, exhausted, and dehydrated.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to maintain hydration or perform basic self-care due to acute physiological exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Visibly tense, diaphoretic, jumping at minor sounds, dark periorbital circles.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia. Abdomen is soft, non-tender, with hyperactive bowel sounds; no peritoneal signs. Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe, palpable spasm and trigger points in the bilateral upper trapezius and levator scapulae muscles. Neck range of motion is significantly restricted due to pain, but Kernig/Brudzinski signs are negative. Oral mucosa is mildly dry, suggesting early dehydration. Findings directly align with excessive uncontrollable worry, severe muscle tension, and profound sleep disturbance.
Environmental Observations: Multiple half-empty cups of herbal tea and various over-the-counter sleep aids scattered on the nightstand.
Medication Reconciliation: No prescribed anxiolytics. Patient has been taking excessive doses of OTC valerian root without effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Generalized anxiety disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute somatic crisis with dehydration)
Differentials:

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, absence of true nuchal rigidity (spasm is muscular and bilateral), and a clear chronic history of anxiety driving the physical symptoms.

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of diarrhea, normal temperature, and the vomiting being explicitly linked to severe panic and nervous tension.

Thyrotoxicosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the chronicity of the worry (6+ months) without weight loss or heat intolerance, though baseline thyroid function testing is warranted outpatient.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute physiological collapse—dehydration, vomiting, and severe painful muscle spasms—driven entirely by severe, unmanaged generalized anxiety and prolonged sleep deprivation. Telephone triage correctly identified the risk of dehydration and the severe neck pain. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was essential to definitively rule out intracranial infection or an acute surgical abdomen, and to safely administer intramuscular or oral antiemetics and anxiolytics. Denying an in-person assessment would have forced an unnecessary emergency department presentation for what is ultimately a severe functional crisis requiring immediate, targeted symptom relief.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Ondansetron 4mg to immediately halt the vomiting and allow oral rehydration. Prescribed a short course of oral Diazepam 5mg at night for up to 3 days to break the severe muscle spasm and force the initiation of a sleep cycle. Expected investigation results (outpatient): GAD-7 assessment scoring indicative of severe clinical anxiety.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Scheduled Worry Time." Instruct the patient to limit active worrying to a specific 20-minute window in the mid-afternoon. If worries arise outside this time, they must be briefly written down and deferred. Reward successful deferral with the physical relief of muscle relaxation (e.g., applying a heat pack). This uses operant conditioning to compartmentalize the rumination, reducing the constant sympathetic nervous system activation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to clinical psychology for Cognitive Behavioral Therapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to assess hydration and initiate a long-term SSRI.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes unable to tolerate any fluids for another 12 hours, or if the neck stiffness becomes rigid and accompanied by a high fever or photophobia.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute somatic symptoms treated; GAD is a chronic condition requiring long-term pharmacological and psychological management.
Clinical Course: The vomiting and muscle spasms will resolve rapidly once sleep is achieved and hydration is restored.

